==English==

===Etymology===
{{unc|en}}. Perhaps from {{suffix|en|band|alt1=band(s)|t1=swaddling clothes|ling}}, or a modification of {{der|en|de|Bänkling||[[bastard#English|bastard-child]]}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|bench|ling}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|UK|_|dialectal}} An [[infant]] or [[young]] [[child]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1809|author=Washington Irving (as Dietrich Knickerbocker)|title=A History of New-York from the Beginning of the World to the End of the Dutch Dynasty|url=http://etext.lib.virginia.edu/etcbin/ot2www-pubeng?specfile=/texts/english/modeng/publicsearch/modengpub.o2w&act=text&offset=774826296&textreg=2&query=+bantling&id=eaf213v1|passage=And I even question whether any tender virgin, who was accidentally and unaccountably enriched with a '''bantling''', would save her character at parlour fire-sides and evening tea-parties, by ascribing the phenomenon to a swan, a shower of gold, or a river god.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1841|author=James Fenimore Cooper|title=The Deerslayer|url=http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Deerslayer/Chapter_1|passage="You!--half-grown, venison-hunting '''bantling'''!..."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=0cXJeqzy6VQC|title=The Wedding Gamble|page=104
|passage="As if he'd let a cow-handed '''bantling''' like you handle them," Cecily muttered.<br>"Children!" Meredyth protested, her face flushing. "What must Lord Englemere think, to hear you brangle so?"}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[bastard#English|bastard-child]].
# {{lb|en|archaic|derogatory}} A [[brat#English|brat]].

